[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The outage is caused by an incorrect firewall policy on the office edge router (router6). Hosts in the office LAN 172.18.51.0/24 (e.g., pc15 172.18.51.15) can reach their default gateway 172.18.51.254 but cannot reach any external subnets; likewise, other sites cannot reach into this LAN. From pc15: local subnet pings (pc16–pc18) succeed, but pings to 172.16.10.4 or 192.168.200.13 time out. From the router itself, pings to those same destinations succeed, proving routing is fine off-box.

On router6, iptables shows a blanket DROP on the FORWARD chain for all traffic arriving on eth2 (the uplink to router3, 10.50.1.1/24): “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP” along with TCP/UDP-specific drops. Packet counters confirm hits (27 packets dropped). This blocks all return traffic from “outside” entering via eth2 back to the office LAN on eth1, so outbound flows from 172.18.51.0/24 never get replies and inbound flows to that LAN are also denied.

Localization: router6 FORWARD chain, interface eth2 (uplink to router3) and eth1 (office LAN gateway 172.18.51.254). Root cause: misconfigured iptables rules dropping all forwarded traffic on eth2 without permitting established return traffic.

Corrective fix: remove/adjust the FORWARD rules on router6. At minimum, add “-A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT” and allow forwarding between eth1 and eth2 (or flush the DROP rules). This will restore bidirectional connectivity for the 172.18.51.0/24 office. === END ===